","/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/nod-82631c52e1.mp3","/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/plot-d4b20a780a.mp3","/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/stove-c310941d7f.mp3","/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/sweep-c0ae81b741.mp3","/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/tape-3ee138797e.mp3","/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/wed-1cf6659c66.mp3","/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/what-is-the-opposite-of-wet-d4b08a507f.mp3","/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/where-did-the-frog-hide-b1166a67ec.mp3","/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/where-does-grandpa-nap-9a9804c413.mp3","/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/where-was-the-kitten-found-810c1cbe08.mp3","/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/which-word-does-not-have-the-oo-as-in-moon-sound-191ee0581b.mp3","/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/which-word-does-not-have-the-ou-as-in-cloud-sound-446cfa64e2.mp3","/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/which-word-has-the-long-a-sound-d4a7f94df4.mp3","/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/which-word-has-the-long-o-sound-131c0863fe.mp3","/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/which-word-names-a-place-6ad743fb63.mp3","/audio/production/en-US/supplemental/who-built-the-robot-788bbf015f.mp3","0c7239fba6d350bd2952dfa70c44ece00617395475e272dd23842af8d32b7142","63621b11575f3027b218321e6acf7c3779fa8db9545089e071ec538657847410","@antonyms_synonyms:antonym_picture","@antonyms_synonyms:synonym_picture","@prefix_suffix:apply_affix_meaning_in_context","A banana is a fruit.","A bat","A board beside it lists classes with spare places, so visitors can choose something new to join.","A bucket caught the drips from the ceiling.","A cat","A child holds a shovel of soil beside a visible hole; this is a child rather than an adult father.","A child sits on a chair with hands in the lap; no drinking or sun is depicted.","A clock","A closed gate keeps footballs out of the young plants in our school garden.","A crane stood on long legs in the pond, spreading its wings before it flew away. Across the road, a building crane lifted steel beams with a long arm and hook.","A fast car moves quickly. Choose the opposite of fast.","A fish swam under a lily pad. A frog swam over to the pad. A bee rested on the flower and a bird sat on a twig.","A frog is airborne above its shadow with a curved motion trail; no hat or hen appears and nothing suggests heat.","A large task can be tackled in smaller parts. Each other choice explains one episode, while this lesson explains both.","A large task can be tackled in smaller parts. It explains the mistake-to-repair change in both episodes.","A metal clip held the two sheets together so they did not blow away","A neighbor brought the lost puppy home. Its owner wanted to express thanks.","A printed programme names the characters and lists the scenes so families can follow the play.","A rat swam to the island. A duck slept on the shore. A turtle swam away from the island. A snail rested on a stone.","A red mitt fell. We picked it up.","A robin carried dry grass to a branch. It pressed mud around the grass. Then it tucked soft strands inside for its eggs.","A seed fell. Rain came. Roots grew. A leaf appeared.","A sign asks drivers to slow down by the pond. A low fence keeps frogs off the road. A tunnel under the road lets them reach the water.","A simple record can preserve details we may forget. It explains the mistake-to-repair change.","A simple record can preserve details we may forget. The changed action resolves the problem in this episode.","A swan swam under the bridge. A dog swam beside it. A duck swam behind them. A frog sat on the bank.","A tall palm had a ringed trunk and long leaves above the beach. Jo held a shell on her open palm, between her fingers and her wrist.","A tiny root appeared. We drew it in our notebook.","A truck carries the heavy boxes.","A visible guide can support a complicated explanation. It explains the mistake-to-repair change.","A visible guide can support a complicated explanation. The changed action resolves the problem in this episode.","A visitor greeted the host after pinning on a badge.","A wooden post held up one end of the fence. Later, the post arrived in a bag of letters that the carrier left at our door.","A worker tests the swings and checks the climbing frame before the playground opens. If a part is loose, she puts a barrier around that equipment. She writes the fault on a repair sheet for the repair team.","Ada hung the picture list above her own head. The younger children could not see what to collect. She crouched beside them, noticed the shelf in their way, and moved the list down where they could see it. Jin explained the model while holding its opening toward himself. His partner could not see the part he named. Jin moved around to his partner’s side and turned the model. The opening was now visible to both of them.","Add a small pinch of salt.","After bird, move to the left of the next line: made.","After is the first printed word.","After lunch, we planted seeds.","After the chick pecked a hole, it pushed its head out. It rested before it stepped out of the shell.","After the cup tipped depends on the milk spilled; together they form the first complete thought. The child fetched a cloth is the second.","After the cup tipped, the milk spilled. The child fetched a cloth.","After the cup tipped, the milk. Spilled the child fetched a cloth.","After the cup tipped. The milk spilled, the child fetched a cloth.","Allow a change to finish before the next step. It explains the mistake-to-repair change.","Allow a change to finish before the next step. The changed action resolves the problem in this episode.","Along the beds, wide paths leave room for visitors to walk and look without stepping on the soil.","Although the soil was dry depends on the seed grew; together they form the first complete thought. The gardener brought water is the second.","Although the soil was dry, the seed grew. The gardener brought water.","Although the soil was dry, the seed. Grew the gardener brought water.","Although the soil was dry. The seed grew, the gardener brought water.","Amir fixed its sail.","An unhappy child has downturned brows and mouth. Sit and sat are excluded because the child is also sitting; there is no sun, drink or arithmetic.","Anya pulled one handle on a carrying box to check it. When she lifted both handles, the unchecked handle came loose. After repairing it, she tested each handle separately before carrying the box.","Arun crowded the drying rack with wet paintings touching edge to edge. Two pictures stuck together. He moved the next paintings onto separate spaces with gaps between them. They dried without sticking.","At rehearsal, one drum beat means stop. Two quick beats mean turn. When the drums go quiet, the dancers wait for the next signal.","At the drinking station, a high tap leaves room for a bottle underneath. Children can fill their bottles without tipping them sideways. Spilled water runs through holes in a tray instead of collecting on the floor.","At the end, Arun closed the bag.","At the end, Arun folded the flag.","At the end, Bea emptied the jug.","At the end, Bea put away the brush.","At the end, Bo put stamps on the cards.","At the end, Dad folded the blanket.","At the end, Dad put away his bottle.","At the end, Eli drew a butterfly.","At the end, Eve collected the menus.","At the end, Jo put away the hoop.","At the end, Kai folded the blanket.","At the end, Kit took off the hat.","At the end, Kit unpacked her cup.","At the end, Lina paid for the pear.","At the end, May put away the chair.","At the end, Nia took down a photo.","At the end, Noor put the book away.","At the end, Ren put away the cards.","At the end, Tess opened another book.","At the end, Uma put on her gloves.","At the end, bright arrows point back to the car park so people can find their return route.","At the end, the actors started the fan.","At the end, the baker put away the flour.","At the end, the class put away the drums.","At the end, the class rubbed out the line.","At the end, the cyclist put on his helmet.","At the end, the group put away the bags.","At the end, the keeper opened the animal gate.","At the end, the nurse fed the puppy.","At the end, the potter dried the clay.","At the end, the teacher opened the register.","At the end, the visitor took off the badge.","At the named occurrence, crane means a long-legged bird.","At the named occurrence, date means a particular day on a calendar.","At the named occurrence, fan means a machine that moves air.","At the named occurrence, jam means a line of traffic that cannot move freely.","At the named occurrence, palm means a kind of tree.","At the named occurrence, post means letters delivered to a home.","At the named occurrence, pupil means a learner at a school.","At the queried occurrence, \"glass\" means a container for a drink.","At the returns desk, a helper scans each book to clear its loan. Readers can then borrow something else. The returned books go on labelled trolleys so another helper can take them to the right shelves.","At the river bend, a seal swam after a fish. The fish swam under a boat. A gull and a crab stayed on the sand.","At the sports centre, a timetable shows which room each class uses.","Aya packed a painted clay tile while its surface was still wet. The wrapping stuck to the picture. She painted a new tile and left it on the rack until it was dry before wrapping it.","Bea filled the jug after folding the napkins.","Bea lifted a glass of water to her lips and took a drink.","Because the rain stopped depends on the path dried; together they form the first complete thought. The children played is the second.","Because the rain stopped, the path dried. The children played.","Because the rain stopped, the path. Dried the children played.","Because the rain stopped. The path dried, the children played.","Before Amir set his boat in the water, he fixed its sail. The boat floated across the tub. He lifted it out on the other side.","Before checking the address, Bo had sorted the cards.","Before choosing a pear, Lina lifted her basket.","Before folding the cloth, they had drawn a line.","Before putting on his hat, Eli sharpened his pencil.","Before putting on the coat, May had chosen a chair.","Before rolling in the bike, he had hung up his helmet.","Before setting out the chairs, Sal closed the window. Before closing the window, Sal had tested the microphone. At the end, Sal opened the window.","Before sharpening the pencil, he had counted the butterflies.","Before she tied on the tail, Pia folded the paper. Once the tail was on, she took the kite outside.","Before the sun rose depends on the birds woke; together they form the first complete thought. The farmer got up is the second.","Before the sun rose, the birds woke. The farmer got up.","Before the sun rose, the birds. Woke the farmer got up.","Before the sun rose. The birds woke, the farmer got up.","Ben has a dog.","Ben is a name at the start of the sentence; dog is a common noun.","Ben rebuilt the top of his block tower whenever it leaned. It kept leaning because a folded cloth was under one corner of the base. He flattened the cloth and rebuilt the base. The tower stayed upright.","Birds sit in trees.","Blend all three sounds in order: /m/ /a/ /p/ makes map.","Bo heard that a parcel belonged by the side door. He put it beside the playground door, but the collector waited elsewhere. Bo asked which side door was meant, then moved it to the door beside the office. Nia was asked to put the seed tray in a bright place. She chose the sunny outdoor wall, where the rain flooded it. She checked with the gardener and moved it to the bright indoor window the gardener had meant.","Brick. Choose the missing letters.","Burrs cling to a fox as it walks. Winged seeds spin away in the wind. Other seeds float down a stream. Each can start a plant far from the parent plant.","Can we go? Yes, the rain has stopped.","Can you hear that? A bell is ringing.","Can you reach the pears? I can get a stool.","Changes the final phoneme from /p/ to /t/.","Changes the first sound to /f/.","Changes the first sound to /h/.","Changes the first sound to /p/.","Changes the first sound to /r/.","Changes the first sound to /s/.","Changes the initial phoneme from /m/ to /k/.","Changes the middle vowel to /o/.","Check identity instead of relying on a quick resemblance. Each other choice explains one episode, while this lesson explains both.","Check identity instead of relying on a quick resemblance. It explains the mistake-to-repair change in both episodes.","Check unclear directions before choosing what to do. It explains the mistake-to-repair change.","Check unclear directions before choosing what to do. The changed action resolves the problem in this episode.","Check what another person can actually see. It explains the mistake-to-repair change.","Check what another person can actually see. The changed action resolves the problem in this episode.","Choose a tool that fits the particular job. Each other choice explains one episode, while this lesson explains both.","Choose a tool that fits the particular job. It explains the mistake-to-repair change in both episodes.","Choose by the job a thing must do, not its looks. Each other choice explains one episode, while this lesson explains both.","Choose by the job a thing must do, not its looks. It explains the mistake-to-repair change in both episodes.","Chooses a believable museum location that the passage does not give for the bags.","Chooses a believable place for boots that is not their stated location.","Chooses a kitchen location that is not the cake’s stated resting place.","Chooses a location phrase without a subject and main verb.","Chooses a noun phrase without a main verb.","Chooses another fabric wrapping but the passage specifically names a blanket.","Chooses another football role rather than the person named as collecting the bibs.","Ch